 "drivingQuestion": "What does 'a son of any kind' add?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Any child, even a mamzer, exempts.",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "yevamot.22b.6",
   "daf": "22b",
   "sentenceId": 5,
   "he": "מִי שֶׁיֵּשׁ לוֹ בֵּן מִכׇּל מָקוֹם פּוֹטֵר וְכוּ׳. ״מִכׇּל מָקוֹם״ לְאֵתוֹיֵי מַאי? אָמַר רַב יְהוּדָה: לְאֵיתוֹיֵי מַמְזֵר. מַאי טַעְמָא — דְּאָמַר קְרָא: ״וּבֵן אֵין לוֹ״ — עַיֵּין עָלָיו.",
   "en": "The mishna states: With regard to «anyone who has a son of any kind,» that son «exempts» his father’s wife from any levirate bond. The Gemara asks: «What is added» by the phrase: «Of any kind? Rav Yehuda said: It adds» a child who is «a mamzer. What is the reason» that a mamzer exempts a woman from any levirate bond? «As the verse states» with regard to levirate marriage: «“And he has no [ain lo] child”» (Deuteronomy 25:5). The phrase ain lo can be understood as ayyen alav, meaning «investigate him.» That is to say, investigate whether he is indeed childless, because any child, even a mamzer, exempts the widow from a levirate bond."
  }
 ],
